From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cloud Office <me@diegonmarcos.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 06:50:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cloud-office: Text Enhance reads the canvas selection, not
 the blank buffer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Enhance has been a menu item that did nothing. Collabora's Android text input is
a hidden contenteditable that never holds the document text — the real selection
lives in the canvas and in core behind it — so the Cloud Keyboard's Enhance read
an empty buffer and returned silently. Weeks were lost to a feature that looked
present and was not.

Copy is the one operation on this platform that demonstrably gets the real
selection, so this follows copy rather than inventing a second path:

  1. The Edit menu's Text Enhance sets a one-shot flag on Clipboard and sends
     core the same `gettextselection` copy sends. Core answers
     `textselectioncontent:`, and the handler that already routes the Action_Copy
     PostMessage API routes ours the same way — returning before the real
     clipboard is touched, so an enhance cannot destroy what the owner copied.
  2. The answer crosses to the shell over COOLMessageHandler, the bridge already
     carrying SAVE, PRINT and .uno:Paste. The enhancer itself is NOT
     reimplemented: routing, model registry, chunking of oversized input, the
     timeout and the provider API key stay behind ITextTools in the Cloud
     Keyboard. Cloud Office calls enhanceWith(), the method that exists so a
     caller can hold its own settings and send its whole decision — the split
     cloud-mail already made. No key is stored or read on this side.
  3. The result goes back through LOActivity.paste(), the same native call the
     ordinary paste path uses, so core replaces the selection through its own
     paste and the owner can undo it in ONE step. Delete-then-insert would have
     been two.

Writer only. The spreadsheet Edit menu is deliberately untouched: a Calc
selection can span cells and one plain-text rewrite pasted back over it would
scatter across them, which is worse than the feature being absent there.

Plain text in both directions, deliberately: formatting inside the enhanced range
does not survive, because the alternative is letting a model emit arbitrary markup
into a document, and a rewrite that invents a table is worse than one that
flattens a bold word.

Nothing here is silent. No selection, a missing keyboard, no API key, a provider
error, an empty reply and a failed paste each end in a message on screen — the
silence WAS the defect.

The selection is never written to logcat. This fleet uploads logcat from its
diagnostics screens, so a logged selection is a selection that leaves the phone.

Settings also moves into the document's own Edit menu; it was previously reachable
only by closing the document you were editing, which is why nobody found it.
